    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "i've checked the resource usage and the node logs, and everything looks pretty clean on the surface. there aren't any obvious spikes in CPU or memory, and the network latency seems within normal parameters when i look at the metrics. it's just these random, bite-sized failures that vanish as soon as i try to monitor them closely. it's like a ghost in the machine or something. is there any way to implement more granular tracing or maybe some kind of distributed tracing to catch these transient errors as they happen?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In cases like yours, distributed tracing can be helpful in catching transient errors. You can consider using libraries like OpenTelemetry or Jaeger to implement distributed tracing. These tools allow you to collect, process, and analyze propagation data from each service throughout your pipeline, giving you a clearer picture of what's happening when the failures occur.\n\nAlso, you can implement logging with atomicity and consistency, using OpenAPI-style logging, or directly using the Jenkins logging plugin with annotations, to track specific pipeline stages and parameters. This might help you identify temporary configuration issues or changes to environment variables.",
      "source": "target",
